a divides b (a|b) if b = ak for some integer k. A prime has exactly two divisors (1 and itself); 1 is neither prime nor composite. The Fundamental Theorem: every integer > 1 factors uniquely into primes. Worked example (verified): factor 360. 360 = 36×10 = (2²×3²)×(2×5) = 2³·3²×5. Check: 8×9×5 = 360 ✓. Divisibility tests: by 3 iff digit sum divisible by 3 (360: 3+6+0=9 ✓); by 11 via alternating sum. Trial division up to √n decides primality for small n: to test 97, trial primes ≤ √97 ≈ 9.8, i.e., 2,3,5,7 — none divide, so 97 is prime. Cryptography’s asymmetry lives here: multiplying primes is trivial, factoring the product is (classically) hard.

