gcd(a,b) is the largest d dividing both. Euclid: gcd(a,b) = gcd(b, a mod b), recurse until remainder 0. Worked example (verified): gcd(48,18). 48 = 2×18+12; 18 = 1×12+6; 12 = 2×6+0 ⇒ gcd = 6. Check: divisors of 48 include 6, of 18 include 6; nothing larger divides both ✓. The algorithm runs in O(log min(a,b)) steps — ancient (c. 300 BCE) and still optimal in practice. Extended Euclid also finds x,y with ax+by = gcd(a,b) (Bézout’s identity), which is how RSA computes modular inverses: d with ed ≡ 1 (mod φ(n)). Whenever you need "the largest shared structure" — key sizes, cycle alignment, fraction reduction — Euclid is the tool.

