Non-cryptographic hashing leans on modular arithmetic: Python’s tuple hash, Java’s 31×h+c string hash, and FNV all mix via multiply-and-mod. Worked example (verified): FNV-1a-ish toy on "ab" with mod 2^8: h=0; h = (h⊕2)×33+97 mod 256: start 0⊕2=2? (use offset basis 0 for the toy) — concretely: h=((0×33)+97) mod 256 = 97; h=((97×33)+98) mod 256 = (3201+98) mod 256 = 3299 mod 256 = 227 (256×12=3072; 3299−3072=227) ✓. Different strings can collide (pigeonhole); good hashes spread uniformly. Diffie-Hellman key exchange is Fermat/Euler in action: both sides compute g^(ab) mod p from exchanged g^a, g^b — eavesdroppers face the discrete-log problem. Takeaway: whenever data meets "mod", number theory governs correctness and security margins.

