Sample space S, event A: 0 ≤ P(A) ≤ 1, P(S) = 1. Addition rule: P(A∪b) = P(A)+P(B)−P(A∩B) (subtract the double-counted overlap); for mutually exclusive events the last term is 0. Complement: P(not A) = 1−P(A). Multiplication: P(A∩B) = P(A)·P(B|A); for independent events, P(B|A)=P(B). Worked example (verified): a queue has 30% high-priority tickets (H) and 20% are escalations (E); 12% are both. P(H∪E) = 0.30+0.20−0.12 = 0.38. If instead H and E were independent, P(both) would be 0.30×0.20 = 0.06 ≠ 0.12 — so they are NOT independent (escalations skew high-priority). Two independent deploys each fail with p=0.05: P(both fail) = 0.05² = 0.0025; P(at least one fails) = 1−(0.95)² = 1−0.9025 = 0.0975.

